<title>GERMANY: VW cuts weekend work due to Spain strike.</title>
<headline>VW cuts weekend work due to Spain strike.</headline>
<dateline>WOLFSBURG, Germany 1997-02-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>German carmaker Volkswagen AG said the elimination of two special weekend shifts had allowed it to maintain a normal production schedule for Monday despite the continuing truckers' strike in Spain.</p>
<p>A VW spokesman said the car-maker, Europe's largest, had not yet had to implement shortened work schedules due to parts shortages at its Wolfsburg plant.</p>
<p>The spokesman said a decision about production plans for Tuesday would be announced later on Monday as the impact of the strike by some 300,000 Spanish truckers begins to cut into vehicle production that relies on prompt deliveries.</p>
<p>Some 20,000 workers at Volkswagen could be affected by stoppages at VW's plant in Wolfsburg, where it produces daily some 3,000 Polo and Golf model car.</p>
<p>--John Gilardi, Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
